1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:00,980
Why do we lie,

2
00:00:00,980 --> 00:00:02,560
even when telling the truth

3
00:00:02,560 --> 00:00:05,430
wouldn't cost us anything?

4
00:00:05,430 --> 00:00:07,800
Think about a normal Monday morning.

5
00:00:07,800 --> 00:00:09,540
You're standing by the coffee machine,

6
00:00:09,540 --> 00:00:11,040
still half asleep,

7
00:00:11,040 --> 00:00:13,300
and a coworker looks over and asks,

8
00:00:13,300 --> 00:00:15,560
"Hey, how was your weekend?"

9
00:00:15,560 --> 00:00:18,940
Now, if you actually answered honestly,

10
00:00:18,940 --> 00:00:20,630
what would you say?

11
00:00:20,630 --> 00:00:24,220
Maybe the real truth is that you barely left your apartment.

12
00:00:24,220 --> 00:00:26,220
You spent most of Saturday on your phone,

13
00:00:26,220 --> 00:00:28,720
ate dinner straight out of a takeout container

14
00:00:28,720 --> 00:00:30,380
at four in the afternoon,

15
00:00:30,380 --> 00:00:32,480
and went to bed early.

16
00:00:32,480 --> 00:00:34,860
What would happen if you said that out loud?

17
00:00:34,860 --> 00:00:36,500
Nobody's going to fire you.

18
00:00:36,500 --> 00:00:39,870
Nobody's going to judge you for resting on a Saturday.

19
00:00:39,870 --> 00:00:42,280
Most likely, your coworker would just say,

20
00:00:42,280 --> 00:00:46,800
"Honestly, that sounds great," and go back to making their coffee.

21
00:00:46,800 --> 00:00:49,320
Nothing bad happens if you tell the truth here.

22
00:00:49,320 --> 00:00:51,340
And yet, before you even think about it,

23
00:00:51,340 --> 00:00:53,620
you hear yourself say, "Oh, it was nice!"

24
00:00:53,620 --> 00:00:55,520
Got a few things done, caught up on some reading,

25
00:00:55,520 --> 00:00:57,810
went for a walk in the park.

26
00:00:57,810 --> 00:00:59,160
Then you walk back to your desk

27
00:00:59,160 --> 00:01:01,844
and realize you haven't set foot

28
00:01:01,844 --> 00:01:03,940
in a park in three weeks.

29
00:01:03,940 --> 00:01:06,260
Most of us have done some version of this.

30
00:01:06,260 --> 00:01:08,297
Saying "I'm on my way!" when

31
00:01:08,297 --> 00:01:10,770
you haven't put your shoes on yet.

32
00:01:10,770 --> 00:01:13,380
Smiling and pretending you've heard of a band

33
00:01:13,380 --> 00:01:16,610
just so you don't have to stop the conversation.

34
00:01:16,610 --> 00:01:19,100
It doesn't mean you're manipulative.

35
00:01:19,100 --> 00:01:21,540
It's a habit almost everyone shares,

36
00:01:21,540 --> 00:01:25,550
and psychologists have actually measured how often it happens.

37
00:01:25,550 --> 00:01:30,994
Back in 1996, psychologist Bella DePaulo at the University of Virginia

38
00:01:30,994 --> 00:01:35,700
asked 147 ordinary people, college students and working adults,

39
00:01:35,700 --> 00:01:38,540
to keep a small diary for a week.

40
00:01:38,540 --> 00:01:43,110
Every time they misled someone, even slightly, they wrote it down.

41
00:01:43,110 --> 00:01:46,720
On average, people told one or two lies a day,

42
00:01:46,720 --> 00:01:51,110
coming up in roughly a quarter of their daily social interactions.

43
00:01:51,110 --> 00:01:53,215
More than seventy percent of those

44
00:01:53,215 --> 00:01:55,940
lies weren't about hiding something serious,

45
00:01:55,940 --> 00:01:58,650
getting money, or staying out of trouble.

46
00:01:58,650 --> 00:02:01,640
People lied about liking a sandwich that was dry,

47
00:02:01,640 --> 00:02:04,060
about why they were five minutes late,

48
00:02:04,060 --> 00:02:07,650
or about having seen a movie someone brought up at lunch.

49
00:02:07,650 --> 00:02:10,476
When DePaulo asked them afterward if anything bad

50
00:02:10,476 --> 00:02:13,360
would have happened if they'd just told the truth,

51
00:02:13,360 --> 00:02:16,900
most of them said no. Not really.

52
00:02:16,900 --> 00:02:20,780
Sometimes, telling the truth is just more trouble than it's worth.

53
00:02:20,780 --> 00:02:22,660
Not because you're afraid of getting caught.

54
00:02:22,660 --> 00:02:25,680
You just don't feel like explaining yourself.

55
00:02:25,680 --> 00:02:28,192
We're used to thinking that honesty

56
00:02:28,192 --> 00:02:30,560
is simple and lying takes effort.

57
00:02:30,560 --> 00:02:31,555
For a big lie,

58
00:02:31,555 --> 00:02:36,030
making up a whole story under pressure, that's definitely true.

59
00:02:36,030 --> 00:02:40,370
In casual conversation, though, it often works the other way around.

60
00:02:40,370 --> 00:02:42,597
Say a friend texts on Friday evening

61
00:02:42,597 --> 00:02:44,700
asking if you want to grab drinks.

62
00:02:44,700 --> 00:02:46,926
The honest reason you don't want to

63
00:02:46,926 --> 00:02:49,280
go isn't that you're upset with them.

64
00:02:49,280 --> 00:02:51,543
You're just tired, your week was long,

65
00:02:51,543 --> 00:02:55,770
and you've been looking forward to staying home in comfortable clothes.

66
00:02:55,770 --> 00:02:58,068
If you give that unfiltered answer,

67
00:02:58,068 --> 00:03:00,760
you often end up managing their reaction.

68
00:03:00,760 --> 00:03:03,070
They might wonder if you're mad at them,

69
00:03:03,070 --> 00:03:04,630
or try to talk you into it.

70
00:03:04,630 --> 00:03:05,909
"Come on, just for one drink,

71
00:03:05,909 --> 00:03:07,320
it'll get you out of the house."

72
00:03:07,320 --> 00:03:09,551
Now a simple reply turns into a

73
00:03:09,551 --> 00:03:12,790
back-and-forth you don't really want to have.

74
00:03:12,790 --> 00:03:15,068
Compare that to: "I'd love to,

75
00:03:15,068 --> 00:03:17,650
I just have a headache coming on."

76
00:03:17,650 --> 00:03:20,840
Nobody feels rejected, nobody argues with a headache,

77
00:03:20,840 --> 00:03:23,810
and the conversation wraps up in five seconds.

78
00:03:23,810 --> 00:03:27,380
Calling in an easy excuse isn't really about deception.

79
00:03:27,380 --> 00:03:31,060
It's just a way to avoid unnecessary friction.

80
00:03:31,060 --> 00:03:33,180
Avoiding plans makes sense.

81
00:03:33,180 --> 00:03:35,505
What's stranger are the lies that slip

82
00:03:35,505 --> 00:03:37,830
out when we're just making small talk.

83
00:03:37,830 --> 00:03:42,340
Someone mentions a neighborhood restaurant, or a book everybody's talking about,

84
00:03:42,340 --> 00:03:44,030
and you hear yourself say, "Oh yeah,

85
00:03:44,030 --> 00:03:45,720
I've been meaning to go back there,"

86
00:03:45,720 --> 00:03:48,390
even though you've never walked through the door.

87
00:03:48,390 --> 00:03:50,680
You're not even trying to impress them.

88
00:03:50,680 --> 00:03:53,890
You just didn't want the conversation to hit a wall.

89
00:03:53,890 --> 00:03:57,393
Psychologist Robert Feldman at the University of

90
00:03:57,393 --> 00:04:01,480
Massachusetts tested how naturally this happens in 2002.

91
00:04:01,480 --> 00:04:03,659
He paired up strangers, asked them to chat for

92
00:04:03,659 --> 00:04:05,980
ten minutes so they could get to know each other,

93
00:04:05,980 --> 00:04:08,150
and recorded the conversation.

94
00:04:08,150 --> 00:04:11,140
Afterward, he had each person watch the video back

95
00:04:11,140 --> 00:04:15,200
and point out every statement that wasn't completely accurate.

96
00:04:15,200 --> 00:04:19,980
Before watching the tape, almost everyone assumed they'd been totally honest.

97
00:04:19,980 --> 00:04:23,436
Once they saw the footage, around sixty percent realized

98
00:04:23,436 --> 00:04:26,460
they'd told at least one lie in just ten minutes,

99
00:04:26,460 --> 00:04:30,360
averaging nearly three small inaccuracies per conversation.

100
00:04:30,360 --> 00:04:32,880
People exaggerated how much they liked something,

101
00:04:32,880 --> 00:04:36,131
claimed familiarity with things they barely knew,

102
00:04:36,131 --> 00:04:39,980
and nodded along with opinions they didn't actually share.

103
00:04:39,980 --> 00:04:43,275
Most of it happened automatically because nobody wants to

104
00:04:43,275 --> 00:04:45,760
look odd or kill the flow of a normal chat.

105
00:04:45,760 --> 00:04:48,628
If the blunt truth creates an awkward pause,

106
00:04:48,628 --> 00:04:50,910
we smooth it over without noticing.

107
00:04:50,910 --> 00:04:54,009
Once one of those small lies leaves your mouth,

108
00:04:54,009 --> 00:04:55,460
a funny thing happens:

109
00:04:55,460 --> 00:04:58,400
it's almost impossible to walk it back.

110
00:04:58,400 --> 00:05:00,940
Imagine telling a coworker you went hiking over the weekend,

111
00:05:00,940 --> 00:05:03,816
and then two seconds later stopping and saying,

112
00:05:03,816 --> 00:05:06,080
"Wait, I don't know why I said that."

113
00:05:06,080 --> 00:05:09,200
"I didn't go hiking. I stayed home all weekend."

114
00:05:09,200 --> 00:05:12,620
Confessing to a major secret makes sense to people.

115
00:05:12,620 --> 00:05:15,612
Confessing to a meaningless lie about a weekend

116
00:05:15,612 --> 00:05:19,050
walk just makes the whole moment deeply uncomfortable.

117
00:05:19,050 --> 00:05:21,828
So you don't correct it. When they follow up with,

118
00:05:21,828 --> 00:05:23,440
"Oh nice, where did you go?",

119
00:05:23,440 --> 00:05:25,900
you name a trail you drove past last summer,

120
00:05:25,900 --> 00:05:28,014
just to close out a conversation neither

121
00:05:28,014 --> 00:05:29,970
of you even cared about a minute ago.

122
00:05:29,970 --> 00:05:33,740
One casual phrase pulls a second one behind it.

123
00:05:33,740 --> 00:05:37,420
When a white lie is isolated, it seems harmless.

124
00:05:37,420 --> 00:05:40,870
Polite untruths help daily life run smoothly.

125
00:05:40,870 --> 00:05:45,399
In 2016, neuroscientists Tali Sharot and Neil Garrett studied

126
00:05:45,399 --> 00:05:49,780
what happens in the brain during repeated small dishonesty.

127
00:05:49,780 --> 00:05:53,420
Using fMRI scans, they focused on the amygdala,

128
00:05:53,420 --> 00:05:56,207
a region closely tied to the emotional tension

129
00:05:56,207 --> 00:05:59,600
or guilt people feel when doing something uncomfortable.

130
00:05:59,600 --> 00:06:05,160
What they found was interesting. As people repeatedly benefited from dishonesty,

131
00:06:05,160 --> 00:06:08,460
their emotional response to it gradually weakened.

132
00:06:08,460 --> 00:06:11,300
In other words, something that felt uncomfortable at first

133
00:06:11,300 --> 00:06:14,640
could become easier to do over time.

134
00:06:14,640 --> 00:06:18,441
There's also a simpler, everyday cost that deception

135
00:06:18,441 --> 00:06:22,900
researcher Aldert Vrij has studied for years: mental fatigue.

136
00:06:22,900 --> 00:06:25,159
Holding back what you actually think,

137
00:06:25,159 --> 00:06:28,640
or keeping track of two versions of the same small story,

138
00:06:28,640 --> 00:06:31,100
takes real attention.

139
00:06:31,100 --> 00:06:33,511
If you've ever walked away from a long social

140
00:06:33,511 --> 00:06:37,100
gathering feeling strangely drained even though nothing went wrong,

141
00:06:37,100 --> 00:06:41,990
part of that exhaustion comes from quietly editing yourself all evening.

142
00:06:41,990 --> 00:06:45,340
Stopping every white lie isn't realistic,

143
00:06:45,340 --> 00:06:48,491
and being bluntly honest at all times would probably

144
00:06:48,491 --> 00:06:51,400
just make life harder for the people around you.

145
00:06:51,400 --> 00:06:56,686
Most pointless lies happen in the first split second after someone asks a casual question,

146
00:06:56,686 --> 00:06:59,740
before you've decided what you actually want to say.

147
00:06:59,740 --> 00:07:03,500
Simply taking a half-second pause before replying, "Hmm, honestly..."

148
00:07:03,500 --> 00:07:08,300
breaks the automatic response just enough to give you a choice.

149
00:07:08,300 --> 00:07:11,217
When a question doesn't really carry high stakes,

150
00:07:11,217 --> 00:07:13,480
it helps to be a little more ordinary.

151
00:07:13,480 --> 00:07:16,220
The next time someone asks what you did over the weekend,

152
00:07:16,220 --> 00:07:19,156
saying, "Honestly, I didn't do much at all,

153
00:07:19,156 --> 00:07:22,160
and I needed it," almost never lands poorly.

154
00:07:22,160 --> 00:07:25,520
More often than not, the other person looks noticeably relieved

155
00:07:25,520 --> 00:07:28,830
and says, "Yeah, me too."

156
00:07:28,830 --> 00:07:33,550
We tell small, pointless lies because we're trying to look a little busier,

157
00:07:33,550 --> 00:07:36,570
a little more interesting, or easier to talk to.

158
00:07:36,570 --> 00:07:39,090
Everyone ends up protecting an image

159
00:07:39,090 --> 00:07:41,820
nobody was judging that closely anyway.

160
00:07:41,820 --> 00:07:44,993
Usually, real connection doesn't start when

161
00:07:44,993 --> 00:07:48,240
two people trade impressive weekend stories.

162
00:07:48,240 --> 00:07:50,144
It starts the moment someone admits

163
00:07:50,144 --> 00:07:51,940
they stayed home and did nothing,

164
00:07:51,940 --> 00:07:53,932
and makes it safe for the

165
00:07:53,932 --> 00:07:56,800
other person to tell the truth, too.
